=====Usage notes=====
The impersonal pronoun has subject and object form {{m|nb|en}}, and genitive {{m|nb|ens}}. Its synonym {{m|nb|man}} is restricted to the subject position; a text using {{m|nb|man}} still uses {{m|nb|en}} as the object and {{m|nb|ens}} in the genitive. This impersonal use has no feminine or neuter forms.<ref>{{cite-web|url=https://sprakradet.no/spraksporsmal-og-svar/man-og-en-og-du/|title=Man og en – og du|work=Språkrådet|date=2024-01-01|accessdate=2026-10-27}}</ref>

When {{m|nb|en}} substitutes for a particular noun, gender agreement remains possible: ''en liten en'' (“a small one”, masculine), ''ei lita ei'' (feminine), and ''et lite et'' (neuter). The expressions ''en eller annen'' and ''en og annen'' can also precede a noun.

